13
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:20,480
So let's get to a couple of the questions that we had come in recently.

14
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:33,680
Now, this is a question that's been coming in quite a bit this time of year, and uh also through the years, as I've had the holography studio through regular mail and since I've had the Internet Web Zum of holography on the web through email.

15
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:42,400
And that question is what would you recommend for a junior high school or a high school science fair project with holography and lasers?

16
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:46,720
My answer to that is, and listen to this carefully.

17
00:01:46,720 --> 00:02:02,600
My answer to that is if you already have a small holography lab in your science department at either your junior high school or your high school, go for doing a hologram and putting that in the Science Fair project and writing up about it.

18
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,040
It's an excellent choice.

19
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:08,680
It's always a very high score with Science Fair projects across the country.

20
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:15,480
However, if you do not already have the facilities to make a hologram, and by facilities, this is what I mean.

21
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:19,560
You're going to need some sort of isolation system which dampens the vibration.

22
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:30,440
Now, this can either be a small table that you put together yourself using the little neoprene balls that they sell now that you can rest a surface on and do the holograms.

23
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,840
They're very easy, easy to create.

24
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:41,160
Or building a sand table, which sometimes is a real pain to put that up and have to take it down just for Science Fair project.

25
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:49,480
Most science departments at junior high schools and high schools that already have a laser have a very low power laser.

26
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:54,920
You're not going to find a th find a 35 milliwatt laser sitting in a high school somewhere.

27
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:56,200
At least I don't think you are.

28
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,200
I haven't seen any 35 milliwatt lasers out there.

29
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:05,880
What you're going to find are half milliwatt helium neon lasers.

30
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:15,080
And to be quite frank with you, I do not think that you have enough power with a half milliwatt laser to do anything that's going to impress judges at a science fair.

31
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,880
Can you make a hologram with a half milliwatt laser?

32
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:18,360
Sure.

33
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:19,640
I've seen them done.

34
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:31,080
There's small holograms, maybe on those little glass plates that you can buy that are two and a half by two and a half inches, where you cut up a larger piece of film like a four by five inch sheet into a smaller piece.

35
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:37,320
But you're not going to get anything that's going to knock anybody's socks off when they come around to your table to see what it is that you've done.

36
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:49,920
What I recommend people do is if you have a laser already in your science department and you want to do a project, unless you've got the facilities to make a hologram, stay away from holography.

37
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:57,680
Go instead to doing a specific project using just the laser, which is much easier, and you can use a half milliwatt laser.

38
00:03:57,680 --> 00:03:59,520
Here are some of the things that I recommend.

39
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:02,160
Number one, this is a quite popular one.

40
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:06,880
And this answers the question, this would be the question of your Science Fair project.

41
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:10,160
Does laser light change the size or shape of an object?

42
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:11,840
And here's what we normally do.

43
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:23,920
We take an object, a simple object, like a wooden block or something else that can be measured quite easily, and we illuminate that object with white light, and the shadow gets cast onto graph paper.

44
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:29,200
We then mark on that graph paper the size of the shadow that the object makes.

45
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:37,680
Then we take the helium neon laser and we pass that beam through a lens and we illuminate the object one more time, this time using the laser light.

46
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:45,760
We keep our graph paper in the same spot so that we're not changing anything with the setup except for the light source itself.

47
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:54,160
And then once that object is illuminated with the laser light and is casting a shadow onto the graph paper, then we take our measurement again.

48
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:58,240
We see does laser light change the size or shape of an object?

49
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:00,960
And then you have to go on to give your reasons why.

50
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:05,280
Is it really because it's a different light source than the white light source that we used?

51
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:07,520
Or does the lens have anything to do with it?

52
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:15,280
The fact that we're taking that unspread beam and spreading it out and then casting the shadow with the uh diverging beam.

53
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:17,840
This is a very popular uh science fair project.

54
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:19,680
And there's a number of other ones too.

55
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:23,840
For instance, lasers are being used in industry and construction.

56
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:27,760
So you may be able to show a pipe.

57
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:37,480
You can take a laser beam and send it through a pipe and show if the pipe is actually bent in any in any areas and things of that nature from the laser beam passing through on the other side.

58
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:43,160
So there's a number of things that you can do that you already have right in your science department.

59
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:53,560
And the only additional items you would need, especially for the first item that I mentioned, would be a diverging lens, which would take the laser beam and spread it out.

60
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:55,640
So that's my answer on that question.

61
00:05:55,640 --> 00:06:05,000
If you've got a Science Fair project coming up in a nutshell, if you have equipment already to do holography, which is a little bit of an isolation system, you're going to have to have lenses.

62
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,080
And these lenses should be in pretty good shape.

63
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:12,760
This can't be the same type of lens that's being passed around to 40 kids in the class and they've got their fingerprints all over it and things of that nature.

64
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:15,560
These need to be pretty good lenses to do a good hologram.

65
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:17,400
You're going to need front surface mirrors.

66
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:26,040
You can't use mirrors that have the silver on the back because you're going to get a very strong interference pattern once that beam is reflected off that mirror and then pass through a lens.

67
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:27,960
You're going to notice a lot of fringes.

68
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:32,040
You have to have a front surface mirror, and once again it should be in very good shape.

69
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:44,520
So if you don't already have these items, my suggestion to you would be just go with the laser that you have and do a simple project answering a few questions about laser light itself.

70
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,840
Okay, let's move on to our next question.

71
