is 1800-458-3525.

74
00:06:57,480 --> 00:06:59,640
Leave your question on the answering machine.

75
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:02,600
You don't need to leave your name or the area that you're calling from.

76
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:03,720
Just leave your question.

77
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:06,840
We'd love to hear your voice on the show.

78
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,840
You can also send email to Holotalk at HoloWorld.com.

79
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:16,320
We got a question, and this is a common question as well, just like the first one.

80
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:19,840
How much power do I need in a laser to make a hologram?

81
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:21,040
And that's a good question.

82
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:25,120
Number one, power equals dollars.

83
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:27,280
So how much money do you have to spend?

84
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:30,320
That that would have to be the first thing that I would have to ask to you.

85
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:38,080
Half milliwatt laser, I would try and stay away from it if possible because number one, you will be able to do very small holograms.

86
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:46,640
I don't know if you'd be able to move into a split beam setup, but you'd be able to make single beam holograms with the half milliwatt, but you're going to be very small holograms.

87
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:48,480
And there's no room for growth.

88
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:52,880
What if you find out that you really like holography and you'd like to stick with it?

89
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:53,840
What are you going to do?

90
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:57,520
You can't move up to bigger holograms because you don't have the power to do it.

91
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:04,240
When I first got started in holography, I made the initial investment to buy a five milliwatt laser.

92
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:06,320
And I made sure that it was polarized too.

93
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:07,680
That's very important.

94
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:10,320
Although you can do holograms without a polarized laser.

95
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:13,120
You don't need one to be polarized to make holograms.

96
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:23,360
But if you have a polarized beam, you're going to find out that you're going to you're going to save a lot of power as that beam travels through your optical system.

97
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:34,720
You'll find out that with a polarized laser, the beam will travel through the lens with less reflection coming off of that substrate than if you were to have a non polarized laser.

98
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:44,320
So why spend the money for a 5 milliwatt unpolarized laser when you're going to be losing a lot of that power as it travels around the table and going through different lenses.

99
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,960
So get yourself a 5 milliwatt laser.

100
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:49,120
Make sure that it's polarized.

101
00:08:49,120 --> 00:09:09,800
I have done in the past, back in the mid 80s, I did multiple color holograms, four by five inch glass plate holograms using a 5 milliwatt laser, utilizing the uh triethanolamine technique, which is uh pre-swelling the plate, pre pre-swelling the emulsion actually before you do the exposure for each of the individual colors.

102
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:33,000
So not only will you be able to start out and learn holography and do your very simple holograms with a five milliwatt laser, but as time goes on and you want a little bit more of a challenge in your holographic journey, a five milliwatt laser is going to give you enough room for growth that you can start moving into some very interesting projects with holography.

103
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,560
So go with the five milliwatt if you can.

104
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:40,280
If you can't, I know there are a number of different three milliwatts out there, it might be a nice middle of the road.

105
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:44,440
But if I were you, I would stay away from the half milliwatt lasers.

106
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:47,240
They're just not worth the money that you're going to put out.

107
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:52,280
You're going to be disappointed after a while when you just can't move on.

108
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:55,320
Okay, let's move on to our third question.

109
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:56,760
This is an interesting question.

110
00:09:56,760 --> 00:09:59,320
I get I get this in email every so often.

111
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:01,400
I don't give it a lot, but every so often.

112
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:06,520
And that question is I need information on holograms that float in space.

113
00:10:06,520 --> 00:10:18,440
Now I'm not quite sure what the person is asking about here, but my guess is that they're asking about holographic images that just sort of like hang in space and float in front of you.

114
00:10:18,440 --> 00:10:24,440
And unless I haven't seen it, I do not know of any holographic images that just sort of like hover in space.

115
00:10:24,440 --> 00:10:28,440
I know there's a number of different techniques that can be used.

116
00:10:28,520 --> 00:10:33,720
There are lasers that can draw three-dimensional images inside of crystals.

117
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:42,040
There are also ways of projecting images out in space using conventional optics such as collimating mirrors.

118
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:54,560
And this this is the type of technique that's used down in the haunted house that everyone always comes up to me and asks me, oh, I saw the holographic faces in the haunted house ride down in Disney World in Florida.

119
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:56,000
I've not been there myself.

120
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:03,600
I do not know firsthand what this looks like when people are seeing it, but they're thinking that it's a hologram.

121
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:24,560
From my research looking into this, I found out that Disney uses traditional optical techniques to do that, and it's either a slide or some sort of transparency, or a moving image, such as a video tape or film that's ref that's reflected off a collimating mirror.

122
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:30,640
Now, what a collimating mirror does is it will take that light and it will focus it out in space.

123
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:36,160
But keep in mind that this is not a three-dimensional image just because it's floating in space.

124
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:50,160
If you were to stop in your tracks and really examine this image, you'd find out that even though it's hovering there in front of you, and you could probably reach out with your hand and pass your hand through it, the actual image itself is flat.

125
00:11:50,160 --> 00:11:51,680
It's not three-dimensional.

126
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:59,040
If it were someone's face, the nose would not be closer to you than one of the ears.

127
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:01,360
It would all be on the same plane.

128
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:06,080
And just because it's floating in space does not necessarily make it a hologram.

129
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:12,320
Myself, I have never seen a holographic image that was just hovering in space.

130
00:12:12,320 --> 00:12:26,400
Traditionally, you need either a holographic plate to store the information on with a light-sensitive emulsion, which contains the fringe patterns, or you need the film, which does the same thing.

131
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:32,840
Now with the stereograms, there's 180-degree stereograms and there's 360 degree stereograms.

132
00:12:32,840 --> 00:12:33,720
You've seen these.

133
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:35,320
They're fairly popular.

134
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:36,520
I like them quite a bit.

135
00:12:36,680 --> 00:12:47,560
They're originally shot on motion picture film, and as the cylinder turns, it's usually on a little motor, and it has a light at the base that comes up and illuminates the inside of the hologram.

136
00:12:47,560 --> 00:13:01,720
And the image is focused to the center of that cylinder, which makes it appear to float in space in between the in the in between the walls of the cylinder as that cylinder rotates.

137
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:08,280
That's probably as close as you're going to get to seeing an image actually hover in space.

138
00:13:08,280 --> 00:13:15,560
And of course, that's hovering inside a device, not outside in thin air.

139
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:22,680
So my answer to this, and correct me if I'm wrong, you can also call in and correct me any time that you want to or send an email.

140
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:23,480
That's fine.

141
00:13:23,480 --> 00:13:34,840
I do not know of any holograms that you can sit down and just see that object or person or whatever the holograms made of, just hover in space in front of you.

142
00:13:34,840 --> 00:13:46,360
Unless there's something to do with a film or plate that has that holographic information on, and there's a light that's shining on that hologram that's taking the light and actually projecting it out.

143
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:55,320
So I don't know what information to give you on holograms that float in space, but I would have a tendency to tell you to look into other techniques.

144
00:13:55,320 --> 00:13:58,760
It's not a holographic technique at all.

145
